+# Experiment G2 — cut DRed's round-trip tax (seconds → target sub-second)
+
+You are `codex` (gpt-5.6-terra, medium). Work ONLY in this worktree
+(`/Users/chrishafley/projects/sprefa-wt-g2-dred-speed`), crate `v6/sprefa-store`
+(own `[workspace]` — `cd v6/sprefa-store` first).
+
+## The problem (measured, from PERF-REPORT.md)
+
+`cascade::retract_dred` (the Rust-driven round loop) is ~5x slower than
+`cascade::retract` (counting) and grows with scale:
+- DAG 960k:  count 445 ms / 29 stmts  vs  dred-loop 2047 ms / 75 stmts
+- DAG 2.9M:  count 1331 ms / 29 stmts vs  dred-loop 6337 ms / 75 stmts
+
+DRed is correct on cycles (counting is not) so we keep it, but it is too slow.
+Note: the CTE variant `retract_dred_cte` is ALREADY MEASURED SLOWER — do NOT
+pursue CTEs. The cost is the two-pass BFS plus per-round round-trip overhead.
+
+## Root cause to attack (concrete, low-risk)
+
+Read `src/cascade.rs`. Two wastes in the round loops of `retract_dred` (and the
+same loop in `assert`):
+
+1. **The `scalar(&txn, "SELECT count(*) FROM cx_next")` probe every round is a
+   whole extra round-trip** just to test emptiness. `execute_unprepared` returns
+   `ExecResult` with `.rows_affected()`. The `exec` helper THROWS IT AWAY
+   (returns `()`). Change `exec` to return `u64` (rows_affected), and in each
+   loop use the INSERT-into-cx_next's rows_affected to decide termination — when
+   it is 0, break. This removes one synchronous round-trip PER ROUND (there are
+   ~30 rounds across the two DRed passes), with no change to results.
+
+   Caveat: `execute_unprepared` of a MULTI-statement batch reports rows_affected
+   for the LAST statement. The loop bodies do `DELETE FROM cx_next; INSERT INTO
+   cx_next(...) SELECT ...` — the INSERT is last, so its rows_affected is exactly
+   the new-frontier size. Good. Verify by keeping the SQL statement order so the
+   INSERT that fills the next frontier is the final statement in its batch.
+
+2. **Redundant `DELETE FROM cx_frontier; INSERT ... SELECT FROM cx_next` swap
+   each round.** Consider swapping frontier/next by ping-pong (alternate which
+   temp table is "current") to drop two statements per round, OR fuse the
+   frontier refill into the same batch as the cx_next fill. Only do this if it
+   stays correct and measurably helps — measure both ways.
+
+## Stretch (only if #1+#2 land and pass): acyclic fast-path
+
+Counting is correct AND ~5x faster whenever the retracted cone has no cycle. A
+`retract_auto` could cheaply test whether the forward cone is acyclic (e.g. the
+over-delete walk already visits the cone; if no node is revisited via a
+back-edge, it is a DAG cone) and, when acyclic, skip the rederive pass entirely
+(the over-delete alone equals counting's answer on a DAG). Do NOT change the
+semantics of `retract_dred`; add `retract_auto` as a separate method and prove it
+equals `retract_dred` on every agreement case. Mark this clearly as optional.
+
+## Do NOT
+
+- Do not touch `retract` (counting) or `retract_dred_cte` semantics.
+- Do not add dependencies. Do not use CTEs for speed (measured slower).
+- Do not change the public result of `retract_dred` — same survivor set, always.
+
+## Validation (run ALL, paste output into the result doc)
+
+```
+cd v6/sprefa-store
+# 1. correctness unchanged — the whole agreement matrix must stay green:
+cargo test --release 2>&1 | grep -E "test result|FAILED|error\["
+# 2. head-to-head speed, hermetic, BEFORE vs AFTER (same graph):
+cargo build --release --example perf_report 2>&1 | grep -E "^error|Finished"
+DL_MEMCAP_MB=2048 ./target/release/examples/perf_report sqlite-dred-loop 6 160000 0
+DL_MEMCAP_MB=2048 ./target/release/examples/perf_report sqlite-dred-loop 6 480000 0
+```
+Report the `ms=` and `stmts=` from the RESULT lines, before and after your change
+(git stash to get the before number, or read the committed PERF-REPORT.md: 960k
+before = 2047 ms / 75 stmts). Success = fewer statements AND lower ms with
+IDENTICAL out_hash, and `cargo test` fully green.
+
+## Measure honestly (the standing law here)
+
+- The gun stays pointed: every perf run keeps `DL_MEMCAP_MB` set (2048).
+- out_hash MUST be unchanged vs the committed baseline — a faster wrong answer is
+  a failure. The perf_report RESULT line prints out_hash; compare it.
+- Do not trust one run; run each scale 2–3x and report the range.
+
+## Style laws (non-negotiable)
+
+- Descriptive names (`rows_affected`/`frontier_size`, never `n`/`r`).
+- No em dashes in comments. No `eprintln!`. N+1 forbidden (stay set-based).
+- No `provenance`/`substrate`/`load-bearing`/`regime` identifiers or prose.
+
+## Communicate back
+
+Write progress + final outcome to `v6/sprefa-store/EXPERIMENT-G2-RESULT.md` here:
+what changed (files, the exec-helper signature change, statement-count delta), the
+BEFORE/AFTER ms + stmts table at 960k and 2.9M with out_hash equality shown, the
+`cargo test` result line, and whether the acyclic fast-path was attempted. Commit
+on this branch. Do not touch other crates or the main checkout.
